4H,6H2,1-2H3,(H,7,8)\",\n      \"role\": \"target\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Biochemical\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_009\",\n      \"formula\": \"C2H6O\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"ethanol\",\n      \"common_name\": \"ethanol\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/C2H6O/c1-2-3/h3H,2H2,1H3\",\n      \"role\": \"intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_010\",\n      \"formula\": \"C3H6O\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"propan-2-one\",\n      \"common_name\": \"acetone\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/C3H6O/c1-3(2)4/h1-2H3\",\n      \"role\": \"intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_011\",\n      \"formula\": \"C5H10N2O\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"isopropylhydantoin\",\n      \"common_name\": null,\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_012\",\n      \"formula\": \"C5H8O3\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"3-methyl-2-oxobutanoic acid\",\n      \"common_name\": \"\u03b1-ketoisovalerate\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/C5H8O3/c1-3(2)4(6)5(7)8/h3H,1-2H3,(H,7,8)\",\n      \"role\": \"hub_intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_013\",\n      \"formula\": \"H2\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"dihydrogen\",\n      \"common_name\": \"hydrogen\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/H2/h1H\",\n      \"role\": \"starting\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Hydrothermal\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_014\",\n      \"formula\": \"C5H11NO3\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"2-hydroxy-3-methylbutanoic acid\",\n      \"common_name\": \"2-hydroxyisovalerate\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/C5H11NO3/c1-3(2)4(7)5(8)6/h3-4,7H,1-2H3,(H,6,8)\",\n      \"role\": \"intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Hydrothermal\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_015\",\n      \"formula\": \"C2H2O\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"ethynol\",\n      \"common_name\": null,\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_016\",\n      \"formula\": \"CH2O\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"methanal\",\n      \"common_name\": \"formaldehyde\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/CH2O/c1-2/h1H2\",\n      \"role\": \"starting\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"reactions\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_001\",\n      \"name\": \"Electric discharge formation of HCN and isobutyraldehyde\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_001\", \"mol_002\", \"mol_003\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_004\", \"mol_005\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [null],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-350K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": \"true\",\n        \"other\": \"electric discharge; strongly reducing atmosphere\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Plasma-driven radical and recombination chemistry build up HCN and branched aldehydes from methane, ammonia and water.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Directly demonstrated by Miller (1957) and Ring et al. (1972); yields biologically relevant aldehydes and HCN. Environmental limitation: requires local/transient strongly reducing atmosphere.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_002\",\n      \"name\": \"Condensation of isobutyraldehyde with ammonia to imine\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_005\", \"mol_002\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_006\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [null],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-340K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": \"true\",\n        \"other\": \"neutral to slightly basic pH\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Ammonia reacts with aldehyde to form an imine (Schiff base).\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Supported by standard Strecker mechanism (Miller, 1957); spontaneous under mild aqueous conditions.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_003\",\n      \"name\": \"Nucleophilic addition of HCN/imine Strecker pathway\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_006\", \"mol_004\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_007\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [null],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-340K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": \"true\",\n        \"other\": \"neutral pH\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Cyanide attacks imine carbon to produce aminonitrile.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Established prebiotic Strecker sequence (Miller, 1957; Ring et al., 1972).\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_004\",\n      \"name\": \"Hydrolysis of valine aminonitrile to valine\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_007\", \"mol_003\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_008\", \"mol_002\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [null],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"330-350K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": \"true\",\n        \"other\": \"mildly acidic or neutral\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Aminonitrile undergoes hydrolysis (acid- or base-catalyzed) to the corresponding amino acid.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Rate and mechanism established for Strecker products by Miller (1957); hydrolysis proceeds on week timescales.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_005\",\n      \"name\": \"Formaldehyde\u2013ammonia condensation (Formose-like hydrothermal network)\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_016\", \"mol_002\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_005\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\"montmorillonite\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"320-350K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"montmorillonite\",\n        \"aqueous\": \"true\",\n        \"other\": \"wet\u2013dry cycling, mild alkalinity\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Oligomerization and aldol condensation among formaldehyde, ammonia, producing diverse carbonyls including branched aldehydes such as isobutyraldehyde.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Formose/amine chemistry (Fox & Windsor, 1970), supported by catalytic studies (Su, Huber); montmorillonite justified as aldehyde/aminated condensation catalyst.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_006\",\n      \"name\": \"NH4CN plus acetone prebiotic network\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_004\", \"mol_002\", \"mol_010\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_008\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [null],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"310-350K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": \"true\",\n        \"other\": \"concentrated cyanide, ammonia\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Multistep network: cyanide reacts with ketone and ammonia, forming various aminonitriles and through unclear C\u2013C extension/hydrolysis yields valine.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Direct valine detection by Friedmann & Miller (1969), though mechanism of C5 branch is unresolved.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_007\",\n      \"name\": \"Pyruvate\u2013acetone (aldol) to \u03b1-ketoisovalerate\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_010\", \"mol_016\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_012\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\"montmorillonite\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"330-350K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"montmorillonite\",\n        \"aqueous\": \"true\",\n        \"other\": \"wet\u2013dry cycling\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Aldol reaction builds C\u2013C to bridge smaller ketones/aldehydes into branched C5 \u03b1-ketoacid.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Speculative upgrade: formose/aldol chemistry is known; modern metabolism builds \u03b1-ketoisovalerate aldolically. No direct abiotic demonstration for valine skeleton, but plausible with surface/mineral assistance.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_008\",\n      \"name\": \"Reductive amination of \u03b1-ketoisovalerate to valine (Ni/H2)\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_012\", \"mol_002\", \"mol_013\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_008\", \"mol_003\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Hydrothermal\",\n      \"agents\": [\"Ni/SiO2-Al2O3\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"295-300K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"moderate (5 bar H2)\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"Ni/SiO2-Al2O3\",\n        \"aqueous\": \"true\",\n        \"other\": \"pH 8, 72 h\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Ammonia imines react at the \u03b1-keto carbon; Ni surface catalyzes hydrogen reduction to the amino acid.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Directly demonstrated (Kaur et al., 2024); mild H2/Ni conditions produce valine from \u03b1-ketoisovalerate.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_009\",\n      \"name\": \"Bucherer\u2013Bergs-type hydantoin pathway (\u03b1-ketoisovalerate)\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_012\", \"mol_004\", \"mol_002\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_011\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [null],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"295K, then 350K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": \"true\",\n        \"other\": \"bicarbonate, cyanide, ammonia, 21 d + 14 d 80\u00b0C\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Cyanide and ammonia react with ketoacid, cyclize to hydantoin, later hydrolyzed to amino acid.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Extensively demonstrated (Pulletikurti et al., 2022) for this reaction family; \u03b1-ketoisovalerate not tested but highly plausible by analogy.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_010\",\n      \"name\": \"Hydantoin hydrolysis to valine\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_011\", \"mol_003\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_008\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [null],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"340-360K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": \"true\",\n        \"other\": \"basic or near-neutral conditions\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Hydantoin ring hydrolyzes sequentially to yield free amino acid.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Mechanistically established within Bucherer\u2013Bergs (Pulletikurti et al., 2022).\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_011\",\n      \"name\": \"\u03b3-radiolysis of formaldehyde/ammonia/water solution\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_016\", \"mol_002\", \"mol_003\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_008\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\"\u03b3 radiation\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-340K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": \"true\",\n        \"other\": \"dose ~20-90 kGy\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Radiolysis yields highly reactive intermediates from formaldehyde and ammonia; after hydrolysis, liberates valine among a broad suite.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Direct valine production demonstrated by Ishikawa et al. (2024); yields are low and product is racemic.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_012\",\n      \"name\": \"Shock-driven synthesis from reduced gases\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_001\", \"mol_002\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_008\", \"mol_009\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [null],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"1500-1800K/rapid quench\",\n        \"pressure\": \"several GPa\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": \"false\",\n        \"other\": \"single-pulse shock; rapid cooling\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"High-energy shock dissociates gases, induces recombination to a suite of amino acids including valine upon quench.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Direct detection (Bar-Nun et al., 1970); geochemically contested (modern mantle less reducing).\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_013\",\n      \"name\": \"FeS-catalyzed reductive ami