entally. The environment (Surface, with bicarbonate/CN\u207b) matches literature.\",\n      \"approved\": false,\n      \"problematic_reactions\": [\"rxn_007\", \"rxn_008\", \"rxn_012\"],\n      \"suggestions\": [\n        \"Balance hydantoin hydrolysis (rxn_012); specify water addition and carbon flow.\",\n        \"Justify availability and accumulation of \u03b1-ketoisovalerate in plausible setting.\",\n        \"Clarify concentrations and pH needed for realistic Bucherer\u2013Bergs chemistry.\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P5\",\n      \"reaction_scores\": {\n        \"rxn_013\": 0.65,\n        \"rxn_003\": 0.85\n      },\n      \"chemical_feasibility\": 0.65,\n      \"pathway_coherence\": 0.8,\n      \"environmental_progression\": 0.95,\n      \"target_achievement\": 0.8,\n      \"overall_confidence\": 0.74,\n      \"feedback\": \"The gamma-radiolysis pathway is well-justified as a minor valine source on carbonaceous small bodies (Ishikawa et al. 2024). However, the single-step conversion from formaldehyde and ammonia to valine (rxn_013) is not balanced and simplifies an extremely complex product mixture and network, slightly overstating selectivity/yield. The plausibility is sound with literature but mechanistic detail is weak, and actual yields are expected to be very low.\",\n      \"approved\": false,\n      \"problematic_reactions\": [\"rxn_013\"],\n      \"suggestions\": [\n        \"Clarify that this represents net product formation, not a mechanistically closed sequence.\",\n        \"Balance the formaldehyde/ammonia to valine conversion.\",\n        \"Explicitly state minor product status and low selectivity.\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P6\",\n      \"reaction_scores\": {\n        \"rxn_014\": 0.6\n      },\n      \"chemical_feasibility\": 0.6,\n      \"pathway_coherence\": 0.9,\n      \"environmental_progression\": 0.9,\n      \"target_achievement\": 0.85,\n      \"overall_confidence\": 0.71,\n      \"feedback